mment  with clarity and authority on the events  of the day and the events of the past  and you have commentators who could  speak with equal grace on other areas  such as say unraveling the mysteries of  baseball for a lay audience  but you have very few perhaps only one  contributor today who can speak with  authority and clarity and intelligence  on both  [Applause]  we have the pleasure today of hearing ms  goodwin talk about her latest book among  other accomplishments team of rival  rivals the political genius of abraham  lincoln  about which book world had to say this  immense  finely boned book is no dull  administrative or bureaucratic history  rather  it is a story of personalities a  messianic drama if you will  please join me in extending a warm  welcome to doris kearns  goodwin thank you  it is such a great treat to be able to  talk about abraham lincoln after having  lived with him for 10 years all by  myself  i've often been asked what surprised me  the most about the lincoln who emerged  from those years of research  and i think my answer would be his  amazing personality i knew that i  respected him as a statesman i knew that  he was a person that i would feel great  admiration for but i had no idea the  affection that i would eventually feel  for him because he had this  extraordinary gift for storytelling and  a hilarious sense of humor in fact the  stories that he liked to tell are not  quite what you might expect from our  marvel monument my favorite lincoln  story had to do with the fact that when  he's told the story that ethan allen the  great revolutionary war hero went to  britain after the revolutionary war and  the british people were still upset  about losing the war so they decided to  embarrass him a little bit by putting a  picture of george washington in the  outhouse where he would have to  encounter it but he went in the outhouse  and instead of coming out angrily he  seemed to be smiling so they said well  didn't you see george washington there  oh yes he said i think it was the  perfectly appropriate place for him what  do you mean they said well he said  there's nothing to make an englishman  [ __ ] faster than the sight of general  george washington  [Applause]  [Music]  so you can imagine if lincoln told  stories like that and there were legion  of those stories before tough cabinet  meetings it would make people relax  i came to believe that early on lincoln  was aware that he possessed unusual  talents and yet it was so hard in that  bleak childhood of his to figure out how  to learn anything he later calculated he  had only gone to school one year  altogether of formal schooling a few  weeks here a few weeks there because his  father needed him to work on the farm  his father would loan him out to other  farmers to whom he owed credit but he  scoured the countryside for books and he  read everything he could lay his hands  on it was said when he got a copy of  king james bible or aesop's fables or  shakespeare's plays he was so excited he  couldn't eat he couldn't sleep the great  poet emily dickinson once said there is  no frigate like a book to take us lands  away how true for lincoln though he  never would travel to england he went  with shakespeare's kings to that merry  land he went with lord byron's poetry to  spain and portugal literature allowed  him to transcend his surroundings  but there were so many losses in his  early life that he was haunted by death  his mother died when he was nine years  old his only sister a few years later in  childbirth his first love ann rutledge  at 22  moreover when his mother died she didn't  promise him that they would meet up  again in the hereafter she simply said  to him abraham i'm going away from you  now and i shall never return  as a result he was haunted by the  thought that when our earthly life comes  to an end that's the end of us it's dust  to dust  so as he grew older he seemed to adopt a  great consolation an old greek notion  that you could live on if you  accomplished something worthy enough so  that your deeds could be told after your  life so your story could be told after  you died and i came to believe that that  huge ambition which was so much larger  than simply for office or power or  celebrity but rather as he said it the  desire to leave the world a little  better place for his having lived in it  that ambition became his lodestar it  carried him through all the early days  of his life all those difficult times it  carried him through the one significant  depression he had when he was in his  early thirties when three things had  come together to lay him low  he had broken his engagement to marry  todd lincoln not sure he was ready to  get married but knowing how much it hurt  her for his doing so his greatest friend  joshua speed the only intimate friend  he'd ever opened his heart to was  leaving illinois to go back to kentucky  and his political career was on a  downward slide  he once wrote to a friend at that time  saying he was the most miserable man  alive and his friends worried that he  was suicidal so they took all knives and  razors and scissors from his room and  his friend joshua speed came to his side  and said lincoln you must rally or you  will die he said i would just as soon  die now but i've not yet done anything  to make any human being remember that i  have lived and then he expressed this  lifelong desire to somehow accomplish  something so that his story could be  told after he died  and somehow fueled by that worthy  ambition he went back to the state  legislature he eventually won a seat in  congress he tried twice for the senate  lost twice someone else might have said  maybe you better go into another  profession but instead he kept going and  became a dark horse candidate for the  presidency on the republican ticket in  1860  everyone expected seward the governor  and senator from new york to be the  nominee the most celebrated anti-slavery  order of 