lling away  so i finally learned you had to tell a  story from beginning to middle to end  much later read an essay by my heroine  barbara tuchman who said even if you're  writing about a war you have to imagine  to yourself you do not know how that war  ended so you can carry a reader with you  every step along the way so in some ways  i learned that instinctively as a kid  maybe that's where my love of writing  but it's certainly where my love of  history began  oh question over here sorry  yes um  thank you so much for your book i've  already read it and um  i thought you should have won the  pulitzer for this book so thank you very  much  um you're you just said how you learned  storytelling and you have this wonderful  part in the book where you talk of how  lincoln essentially educates himself  and  how as a young man  he um  he tells stories to the other kids  and he like memorizes things but then  kind of translates them into the  language of  how the kids can understand them can you  talk about that a little more i'd be  glad to i mean was one of the things  that so interested me to find out about  lincoln he said when he was only like  seven or eight years old  and he lived in this log cabin which was  on a road where a lot of people would  come by to go to market  and he would listen to them telling  stories with his father his father was a  good storyteller as well and he said he  couldn't sleep when he went up to his  loft at night until he could translate  the stories that he heard into language  that his own friends on the field could  then listen to the next day then he'd go  out in the field and he'd stand on a  tree stump and entertain his little  friends and i think in some ways that  became his first awareness of the  pleasure that an audience can bring but  i think it's also true that storytelling  became for him a way of dealing when you  think of oral history what it is is  connecting the past to the present and  the future and of course in the  gettysburg address he gave america the  greatest story of them all connecting  our past to the president the future and  how this new birth of freedom was going  to create a better america than the  america that they were fighting for over  here another question um  robert todd lincoln he had three  children what did he have whatever  happened to him because the lincoln  family  ended with his grandchildren  you know i don't really know all that  much about robert toddling and i've been  up to his house in hildeen and yes he  did have children but after a period of  time he had daughters and then there  were no more people to carry on the  lincoln name  so that um it's true that the lincoln  line can you imagine if we had lincoln  descendants in today's world it would be  really fun to i'd give anything to talk  to them everybody keeps teasing me what  if you could find a lincoln or abraham  and you could talk to him for five  minutes what would you ask him and i  know as an historian i'm supposed to say  what would you have done with  reconstruction the question we always  ask as historians but i think i'd simply  say to him abraham tell me a story so i  could watch him laughing his face would  come to life his life his eyes would  light up and he would laugh louder than  everybody else one more question over  here i was fascinated by your early  description of lincoln's nickname spotty  lincoln  would you enlighten us  in his what his analysis and thought was  against illegal and preemptive war  what happened is when lincoln was in the  congress he gave his maiden speech on  the mexican-american war  what had happened was he argued that  president polk had actually started the  war for reasons that he didn't tell the  public that the reasons he wanted the  war was because he wanted more territory  to come into the united states and the  president he claimed had falsely argued  that the mexicans had attacked us on our  land rather than we attacking the  mexicans