n hold the soft cover in bed  it's not as heavy as the hardcover  otherwise you know it's it's pretty much  the same what has this experience been  like for you now on book tour for about  a year  well you know after you spend 10 years  writing a book and you're solitarily at  home doing it it's such a great treat to  talk to the people who've read it to  tell stories about it and and i've loved  every minute of it  10 years researching abraham lincoln and  i know you're often asked the question  what you learn most about the man what  lessons can political leaders take from  lincoln's time to what we're seeing  today in america i think the kind of  public leader he was is somebody that  everybody could learn from i mean he had  the greatest empathy for understanding  where other people were coming from  listening to other people's point of  view  not disparaging his rivals being able to  share credit when success was there  shouldering responsibility for failure  acknowledging errors when he made them  you know we have so many politicians  today who seem to think they have to say  the same thing they said 20 years ago 30  years ago he would say i've changed my  mind on that i'd like to believe i'm  smarter today than i was yesterday so i  think if we had this character around  today we'd be a much better nation we'll  let you go to the book signing doris  korn's goodwin thank you very much for  joining us again so welcome it's great  to see you again  questions  is  [Music]  thank you  i'll be down there  [Applause]  [Music]
 

